# Backend Architecture

Use:

```text
Presentation -> Application -> Domain
Infrastructure -> Application/Domain ports
```

## Domain

Domain code may contain:

- entities
- aggregate roots
- value objects
- domain services
- domain errors
- domain events
- repository interfaces

Domain code must not import:

- NestJS
- MikroORM
- Prisma
- Redis
- BullMQ
- HTTP clients
- framework decorators
- environment/config modules

## Application

Application services/use cases:

- authorize
- validate command-level rules
- load aggregates
- invoke domain behavior
- coordinate transactions
- call ports
- create outbox records
- map domain results to application outputs

Application code must not contain raw SQL or provider-specific HTTP details.

## Infrastructure

Infrastructure implements ports:

- MikroORM repositories
- Redis adapters
- queue adapters
- payment adapters
- S3 adapters
- Meilisearch adapters
- email/SMS adapters

## Presentation

Controllers:

- authenticate request
- validate DTO
- resolve actor/context
- invoke a use case
- map result to HTTP response

Controllers must not contain business rules.

## Module boundaries

A module may access another module only through its published application contract/port.

Do not import:

```text
other-module/internal/domain/*
other-module/infrastructure/*
other-module/presentation/*
```

Cross-module operations should use:

- application ports
- commands/queries
- domain events
- integration events

Avoid a global "shared service" that becomes a hidden dependency graph.

## Aggregates

- Mutate aggregates through methods expressing business intent.
- Do not expose mutable collections.
- Do not add setters merely to satisfy ORM persistence.
- Use factories for invariant-heavy creation.
- Prefer domain-specific errors over generic errors.

## Repository interfaces

Repositories belong to the domain/application boundary.

Example:

```ts
interface OrderRepository {
  findById(id: OrderId): Promise<Order | null>;
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
}
```

MikroORM implementation belongs in infrastructure.
